\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s11 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description \
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Instances of the NSCursor class manage the appearance of the cursor. When you initialize an cursor\'d0the designated initializer is \b initWithImage:\b0 \'d0you assign it a 16-by-16 pixel NSImage. Later, if you want to change the image, you can use \b setImage:\b0 . The image is usually an small, opaque icon\'d0for example, a pair of cross-hairs\'d0surrounded by transparent pixels. The pixels in the cursor image are mapped on a flipped coordinate system:  the upper left pixel is (0,0); the lower right is (15,15).\
\fs14\
\fs28 An application may use several cursor instances\'d0for example, one that looks like an arrow and one that looks like an I-beam. The instance that currently appears on the screen is called the \'aacurrent cursor,\'ba and is referenced by the currentCursor class method. You can make a cursor current in several ways:\
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab Most simply, you can send a cursor a \b set\b0  message. This message makes the receiver current.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab You can manage cursors in a stack, using the \b push\b0  and \b pop\b0  methods. The stack\'27s top cursor is current.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab You can tell a cursor to become current when the mouse enters a part of the screen known as the \'aacursor rectangle.\'ba To do this, you first assign the cursor to an NSRect, using NSView\'27s \b addCursorRect:cursor:\b0  method:\
\fs14\
\pard \s21 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 [aView addCursorRect:&aRect cursor:aCursor];\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \tab This assignment means that when the mouse enters \i aRect\i0 , \i aCursor\i0  will receive a \b mouseEntered:\b0  event message. Next, using NSCursor\'27s \b setOnMouseEntered:\b0  method, you tell \i aCursor\i0  to respond to the \b mouseEntered:\b0  event by setting itself\'d0that is, by making itself current:\
\fs14\
\pard \s21 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 [aCursor setOnMouseEntered:YES];\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \'b7\tab Conversely, you can tell a cursor to set itself when the mouse exits the cursor\'27s rectangle, using the \b setOnMouseExited:\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 To determine when exactly the mouse is inside a particular cursor rectangle, the Application Kit tracks a single pixel in the cursor image. This pixel is known as the \i hot spot\i0 , and you can reference it using the \b hotSpot\b0  method. By definition, the location of the current cursor\'27s hot spot is the location of the mouse; when the hot spot is inside a cursor rectangle, so is the mouse.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The hot spot is useful not only for determining which cursor is current, but for determining where a mouse click should have its effect. You should usually set the hot spot, using \b setHotSpot:\b0 , to the point where users will focus their attention. In a cursor that looks like cross-hairs, for example, you should set the hot spot at the crossing point.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The Application Kit provides two ready-made cursors for commonly used cursor images. You can retrieve these cursors by using the \b arrowCursor\b0  and \b IBeamCursor\b0  class methods. There\'27s no NSCursor instance for the wait cursor, because the system automatically displays it at the appropriate times.\
\fs14\
